      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 113,
      "outputTokens": 745,
      "costUsd": 0.0067615,
      "pronunciationText": "Think about what a primitive means here. We want a curve whose gradient, at each x, equals the height of one over x. For positive x, we already know one. The derivative of the natural log of x is one over x. At x equals two, the log curve has gradient one half, the same as the height of one over x at two. But one over x has a second piece on the left, where x is negative. The natural log of x doesn't exist there, so it can't be the whole answer."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-i04/scene-03.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1127,
      "worstWordGap": {
